<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#damel">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Damel</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1549–1886</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Damel was the title of the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolof">Wolof</a> kingdom on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. The first Damel, Amari Ngoone Sobel Fall, threw off the authority of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire</a> in 1549, and later holders were chosen from royal lineages by a council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>. The title ended with the French conquest in the 1880s; its best-known holder is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior Diop</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/damel.jpg" alt="Damel" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ji-Elle · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AmadouMakhtarMbayeBox.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
